{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And you shall make an altar for burning incense.</b> As it appears, these two altars were both set up to atone for the sinning soul. For the bronze altar comes to atone for the parts of matter and the body that is stricken by the stone of sin, and upon it they offer the living creatures that come as its substitute, for they have resemblance to the matter of man. And the form of the altar proves it, for its height resembled the height of an average man — three cubits — and likewise the animals offered resemble the bestial soul within man, a soul in substitution for a soul. For on this account it is said of the poor man's meal offering, ״וְנֶפֶשׁ כִּי תַקְרִיב״ (Leviticus 2:1), for he does not have the means to offer the soul of an animal as a substitute for his own soul, so he brings a meal offering of fine flour; therefore Scripture says ״וְנֶפֶשׁ״ — I account it to him as though he offered his own soul." "Nevertheless, the soul too needs atonement, after it has been defiled in this stricken body, and it is not atoned for by the soul of an animal, for there is no resemblance of this to that — for רוּחַ בְּנֵי אָדָם עוֹלָה לְמַעְלָה וְרוּחַ הַבְּהֵמָה יוֹרֶדֶת לְמַטָּה (Ecclesiastes 3:21), and how can the soul of an animal, which is consumed and perishes, be a substitute for the soul of man, which endures forever? Therefore El Ḥai commanded making the altar of incense, which raises smoke and a sweet savor to HaShem, to atone for the spirit of the children of man, which ascends upward like the smoke of the incense — and it too is perfumed with מוֹר וּלְבוֹנָה (Song of Songs 3:6), of good deeds. And to instruct us, He placed in our hearts what is written (Exodus 30:36), ״וְשָׁחַקְתָּ מִמֶּנָּה הָדֵק״ — an allusion to the soul, which is finer than fine, that it too needs atonement in order to raise it to the place from which it was hewn. And the number of cubits of this altar proves it, for concerning it is said ״אַמָּה אָרְכּוֹ וְאַמָּה רָחְבּוֹ״ (Exodus 30:2) — cubits in the singular, to atone upon it for the soul, which is called יְחִידָה, for just as the Holy One, blessed be He, is One, so too it is יְחִידָה. ״וְאַמָתַיִם קֹמָתוֹ״ (Exodus 30:2), for the spirit of the children of man ascends upward to its place in scarcity of the space it has within the body. And the time of its burning of incense is morning and evening, for the soul in the morning comes in its youth, the time of the shining of its sun, and in the evening it returns to its Father as in the days of her youth, and would that its departure be like its arrival without sin. And the incense comes ״בְּהֵיטִיבוֹ אֶת הַנֵּרוֹת״ in the morning (Exodus 30:7), and in the evening ״בְּהַעֲלֹת אֶת הַנֵּרוֹת״ (Exodus 30:8), and the vision of evening and morning is true, for it relates to ״נֵר ה׳ נִשְׁמַת אָדָם״ (Proverbs 20:27). For the morning is the time of trimming the lamp of Elohim, the soul of man, to set its deeds aright and to cleanse it from all rust of ash, and the evening, the time of the sun's setting, is the time of the raising of the soul to the place from which it was hewn — and this burning of incense comes to atone upon the soul so that its departure from the world be without sin, as is its arrival." "And the golden crown of the altar — this corresponds to the reward of the righteous in the World to Come, who delight in the splendor of the Shekhinah, ״וְעַטְרוֹתֵיהֶם בְּרָאשֵׁיהֶם״, and it does not say ״עֲטָרוֹת״ but rather ״וְעַטְרוֹתֵיהֶם״ — an allusion to those crowns they acquired at Mount Sinai when they said ״נַעֲשֶׂה וְנִשְׁמָע״, as is found in tractate Shabbat, chapter Rabbi Akiva (88a). And it concludes there, ״בְּחוֹרֵב טְעָנוּם וּבְחוֹרֵב פְּרָקוּם״, and Resh Lakish said, ״עָתִיד הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא לְהַחֲזִירָם לָהֶם״, etc. Therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 17a), ״וְעַטְרוֹתֵיהֶם בְּרָאשֵׁיהֶם״ — those crowns that were taken from them, and He is destined to restore them. And for this reason it is said here, ״וְנָתַתָּ אוֹתוֹ לִפְנֵי הַפָּרֹכֶת אֲשֶׁר עַל אֲרוֹן הָעֵדֻת לִפְנֵי הַכַּפֹּרֶת אֲשֶׁר עַל הָעֵדֻת״ (Exodus 30:6) — that is, positioned opposite the Ark from outside. And all this comes to instruct that the crown of the altar of incense alludes to ״וְעַטְרוֹתֵיהֶם בְּרָאשֵׁיהֶם״, that is, the crown of Torah and the crown that was given them at Horev and removed from them, and He is destined to restore them to them."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {2 "The phrase וַעֲטָרוֹתֵיהֶם בְּרָאשֵׁיהֶם is a Talmudic paraphrase (e.g. Berakhot 17a, Shabbat 88a) not found verbatim in Scripture; kept in Hebrew without verse address as the author cites it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:24.297383085Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}